y and $67M-to-$3M cash advantage", "October polls show a tight 2-3 point race in a Democratic-friendly environment", "Texas's R+6 partisan lean caps Hinojosa's ceiling", "Markets and most raters still favor Abbott despite close polling"], "rationale": "October surveys show Abbott ahead by about two points on average, with a few polls even putting Hinojosa in front, but his incumbency, overwhelming financial edge, and Texas's Republican lean keep him favored; prediction markets price Hinojosa's chances near one in four.", "watch": "If late-deciding voters continue breaking toward Hinojosa or the Musk-related lawsuit reshapes the race's closing message, this could tighten into a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Greg Abbott (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor Greg Abbott seeks another term against challenger Gina Hinojosa in a race that has tightened considerably. Mid-October polls show Abbott ahead by two to four points, with some surveys putting Hinojosa narrowly in front, and the statewide polling average sits near Abbott plus two. That is far closer than the state's six-point Republican lean would suggest.\n\nAbbott still holds the structural advantages: he has raised roughly $25 million and holds $67 million on hand against Hinojosa's $3 million, and most race raters call the contest Lean Republican. Prediction markets give Hinojosa roughly a one-in-four chance. A favorable national environment for Democrats keeps the race competitive, but the incumbent's resources and Texas's underlying lean make him the favorite as early voting begins.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1819, "margin": -4.27}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2694, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2894, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2702, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2293, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2331, "margin": -3.3}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2978, "mar